# Docker ignore patterns are not recursive: `node_modules/` matched only a top-level
# `node_modules`, never a nested one. See the backend `.dockerignore` for the measured
# consequence of the same mistake on that context
# (docs/security/audit/run-1/verification/containers/images/images.md, section 5b).
#
# NOTE on the limits of this file. The runner stage is `COPY --from=builder /app ./`
# (the last stage of frontend/Dockerfile), and `.dockerignore` does not apply to a stage-to-stage copy. The
# patterns below therefore work in one direction only: they keep a path out of the BUILDER (whose
# context copy at Dockerfile:9 they do govern), and a path the builder never received cannot
# reach the runner. What they cannot touch is anything the builder acquired by other means --
# above all the 824.8 MB dev+prod `node_modules` that arrives via `COPY --from=deps`
# (Dockerfile:8) and then crosses into the runtime image whole. Pruning that needs a Dockerfile
# change (a production-only install, or Next's standalone output), not a pattern here.

# Dependencies (the deps stage installs its own)
**/node_modules/

# Build and test output
.next/
out/
**/coverage/
**/*.tsbuildinfo

# Secrets
.env
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/.env.*.local

# Not reachable from this context today, excluded so it stays that way if the context moves.
.git/
.gitignore

# Agent tooling config. `frontend/.claude/launch.json` was confirmed present at
# `/app/.claude/launch.json` inside the runner image; it is local developer configuration and has
# no business in a shipped image (images.md section 5d).
.claude/
